---
title: "Dependent Machine-Failure Process"
subtitle: "An AR(1)-style dependence stress test"
format: html
---

```{r}
source("../R/helpers.R")
student_id <- 6599L  # Replace with the last four digits of your student ID.
B <- B_DEFAULT
study <- study_population("dependent_machine", seed = student_id, B = B)
d <- study$diagnostics
```

## Population and prediction

Because the handout describes this process as “coming soon,” this analysis
records an explicit assumption: I simulate a Gaussian AR(1) process with
(
ho=0.80), then transform it so each marginal observation is exponential.
The dependence is positive, so adjacent failures carry information about one
another. I predict slower convergence than for independent exponential data.

## Experimental design and decision rule

I generated `r paste0("B = ", B)` dependent sample means at candidate sizes `r paste(N_VALUES, collapse = ", ")`. I apply the same skewness, kurtosis, Q-Q correlation, and Q-Q
RMSE thresholds as elsewhere, requiring three consecutive passes. The simulated
standard deviation is compared descriptively; the independent formula
\(1/\sqrt{n}\) is not used because dependence changes the variance of a mean.

## Results

```{r}
display <- d[, c("n", "simulated_mean", "simulated_sd", "skewness",
                 "excess_kurtosis", "qq_correlation", "qq_rmse",
                 "normal_enough", "stable_pass")]
display$normal_enough <- ifelse(display$normal_enough, "yes", "no")
display$stable_pass <- ifelse(display$stable_pass, "yes", "no")
knitr::kable(display, digits = 3)
```

## Visual evidence

```{r}
plot_n <- if (is.na(study$selected_n)) max(N_VALUES) else study$selected_n
x <- study$samples[[as.character(plot_n)]]
old_par <- par(mfrow = c(1, 2))
hist(x, breaks = 40, probability = TRUE, col = "goldenrod", border = "white",
     main = paste("Dependent means: n =", plot_n), xlab = "Sample mean")
qqnorm(x, main = paste("Normal Q-Q plot: n =", plot_n), pch = 16, cex = 0.3)
qqline(x, col = "firebrick", lwd = 2)
par(old_par)
```

## Conclusion

The first stable pass is **`r ifelse(is.na(study$selected_n), "no stable value in the investigated range", paste0("n = ", study$selected_n))`**. Positive dependence reduces the effective amount of independent information, so the same nominal \(n\) should be interpreted as less informative than \(n\) independent observations. If the instructor supplies a different machine-failure generator, I will replace the generator while preserving the design and criteria.

## Reproducibility check

```{r}
reproducibility_check("dependent_machine", n = plot_n, seed_a = student_id,
                      seed_b = 2026L, B = B)
```

